[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - The summary doesn't mention that exponential speedup applies only to **specific problem classes**, not all problems
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - No mention of current limitations: decoherence, error rates, limited qubit counts, and the difficulty of scaling
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - "Solving certain complex problems" is vague—many important problems don't have known quantum speedups
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m ⚠️ **Practical gaps**:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Current quantum computers are in early stages (NISQ era—Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum); practical advantage over classical computers is not yet established for most real-world applications
