[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m # Weakness: Lacks Discussion of Practical Limitations
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m The summary presents quantum computing's advantages without acknowledging significant barriers to implementation. It doesn't mention:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Decoherence and error rates**: Qubits are extremely fragile and lose their quantum properties quickly, requiring error correction that demands many more qubits than theoretically needed
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Current scalability constraints**: Existing quantum computers have only dozens to hundreds of qubits, far below what's needed for many "exponentially faster" solutions
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Limited practical applications today**: The promised breakthroughs in cryptography, drug discovery, and optimization remain largely theoretical; few real-world problems have been demonstrably solved better on quantum computers than classical ones
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59865, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m This creates an incomplete picture that may overstate how soon and how broadly quantum computers will deliver on their theoretical potential. A balanced summary would acknowledge both the promise and the substantial engineering/scientific hurdles that remain.
