[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m **2. Current practical limitations (unverified if implied as current state)**
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Existing quantum computers are in early stages (NISQ era—Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Error rates remain high; quantum advantage isn't yet demonstrated for most real-world problems
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Scaling to problem-solving scale requires significant advancement
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m **3. Cryptography claim needs clarification**
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Quantum computers would *break* certain encryption (like RSA)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - But they could also *enable* new cryptographic methods (quantum key distribution)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - The relationship is more complex than stated
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Trade-offs: requires specific problem encoding; not universally faster
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Decoherence and error correction challenges
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65111, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Current state vs. theoretical potential
