[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m **"Immutability" is overstated:**
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Records are *practically* difficult to change, but not theoretically impossible
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - A majority attacker could theoretically rewrite history (51% attack)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Immutability depends on network size and security assumptions
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m **Not universally "ideal":**
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Transparency and immutability create privacy challenges in some use cases
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Energy-intensive consensus mechanisms (Proof-of-Work) raise sustainability concerns
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Many applications work better with traditional databases (faster, cheaper, simpler)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Trust without central authority only works when the network itself is trustworthy
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m **Context matters:**
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Some blockchains are actually *centralized* (few validators)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - Private blockchains sacrifice decentralization for control
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m - The "trust" benefit only applies to situations where you don't already have institutional trust
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=65109, ip=10.0.10.142)[0m ## Verdict
