[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m ## Important Nuances & Caveats ⚠️
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 1. **"Vast numbers of possibilities"** — While true, this needs qualification:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - The advantage is problem-dependent; not all problems benefit equally
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - Speedup requires special quantum algorithms (not automatic)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 2. **Practical limitations not mentioned**:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - **Decoherence**: Qubits lose their quantum state quickly (milliseconds to seconds currently)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - **Error rates**: Current quantum computers have high error rates (~0.1-1% per operation)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - **Scalability challenges**: Scaling to thousands/millions of qubits remains difficult
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m    - **NISQ era**: We're still in the "Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum" phase; no definitive quantum advantage for practical problems yet
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=59864, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 3. **Cryptography claim needs context**: Quantum computers threaten *current* encryption but aren't universally faster for all cryptographic tasks
