[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m **Oversimplification of practical applicability**: The summary suggests quantum computers are broadly revolutionary across multiple fields, but doesn't acknowledge that this advantage only applies to *specific problem types*. It implies quantum computers will be faster at solving "complex problems" in general, when in reality:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Most everyday computing tasks show no quantum advantage
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Quantum speedup requires problems with particular mathematical structures (like factoring or database searching)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - For many real-world applications in cryptography, drug discovery, and optimization, we're still in early experimental stages—not at deployment
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Error rates and decoherence currently limit practical performance
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
