[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62642, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Doesn't mention that quantum advantage only applies to *specific problem types* (not all problems), which could mislead readers into thinking quantum computers are universally superior
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62642, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Omits key challenges like quantum decoherence, error rates, and the difficulty of building stable qubits—factors that currently limit quantum computers' practical utility
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62642, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Doesn't note that we're still in early stages; most quantum computers today have relatively few qubits and high error rates, making them unsuitable for many proposed applications
