[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Quantum computers today are still in early developmental stages with limited qubit counts
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Qubits are extremely fragile and susceptible to "decoherence" (losing their quantum properties)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - Most quantum computers require near-absolute-zero temperatures to operate
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m - We don't yet have quantum computers that clearly outperform classical computers on practical, real-world problems
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60942, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m This omission creates an inflated impression of quantum computing's immediate utility, when in reality the field remains largely experimental with uncertain timelines for widespread practical application. A more balanced summary would acknowledge both the theoretical promise *and* the formidable engineering challenges that currently prevent quantum computers from delivering on that promise.
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64311, ip=10.0.10.175)[0m ## Key Improvements Made:[32m [repeated 3x across cluster][0m
