[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m - **Potential speedup**: Quantum computers can theoretically achieve exponential speedups for *certain* problem classes
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m - **Application areas**: Cryptography, drug discovery, and optimization are legitimate research directions
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m ## ⚠️ Important Qualifications
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m **"Exponentially faster for certain problems"** — This is more precise than general speedup claims:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m - Speedups are proven only for *specific* problem types (factoring, discrete logarithm, quantum simulation)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m - Many problems show no known quantum advantage
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m - The practical speedup depends heavily on implementation and error rates
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60699, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m 
