[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Secure transaction recording**: Verified—cryptographic hashing makes tampering detectable
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Multiple computers/decentralized**: Accurate—transactions are validated across a network of nodes
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Resistance to tampering**: Valid—altering past blocks would require recalculating subsequent blocks across the majority of the network
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Transparent record**: Correct—all transactions are visible to network participants
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m ## Nuances Worth Adding
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **"Resistant to tampering" is conditional**: Security depends on network size, consensus mechanism, and computational power. Smaller blockchains with fewer nodes are more vulnerable.
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **Transparency trade-off**: While transactions are visible, user identities can be pseudonymous (not truly anonymous), and some blockchains offer privacy features.
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64065, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - **"All participants can verify"**: True in principle, but verification requires technical knowledge and access to full node data; most users rely on simplified clients.
