[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62380, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 1. Quantum computers aren't universally faster—they excel at specific problem types (factoring, optimization, quantum simulation) but offer no advantage for many everyday computing tasks
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62380, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 2. Readers are left without concrete examples or understanding of real-world applications
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62380, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 3. The vague phrasing ("certain complex problems") could mislead readers into thinking quantum computers are broadly superior to classical computers across all domains
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62380, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=62380, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m A stronger summary would specify problem categories like cryptography, drug discovery, or combinatorial optimization to give readers a clearer picture of quantum computing's actual scope.
