[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64834, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=64834, ip=10.0.10.50)[0m The summary presents quantum computers as remarkably powerful but fails to mention that this advantage only applies to *specific problem types*. It doesn't clarify that quantum computers are not universally faster—they excel at particular tasks (like factoring large numbers or simulating molecular behavior) while remaining no better than classical computers for everyday tasks like word processing or browsing.
