[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m **Lack of practical context or limitations:** The summary presents quantum computing's capabilities in an overly optimistic light without acknowledging significant real-world constraints. It doesn't mention that:
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Quantum computers are still largely experimental and not yet practically superior to classical computers for most real-world applications
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Qubits are extremely fragile and prone to decoherence (losing their quantum properties)
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - Current quantum computers have very limited qubit counts and high error rates
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m - The problems quantum computers can solve "exponentially faster" represent a narrow class of specific problems, not general computing tasks
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m 
[36m(_strands_fanout_task pid=60730, ip=10.0.10.235)[0m This omission creates a misleading impression that quantum computers are a near-term replacement for classical computing, when the reality is far more nuanced.
